language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_074_maximum_clique: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Given a simple undirected graph G = (V, E), a clique is a subset of vertices in which every two distinct vertices are adjacent (connected by an edge). Find a clique of the largest possible size in the graph.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_074_maximum_clique.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_074_maximum_clique.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_074_maximum_clique_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_074_maximum_clique.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_074_maximum_clique_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the number of vertices
given n : int(1..)
$ adj[i,j]: 1 when vertices i and j are joined by an edge, 0 otherwise (vertices indexed 1-based, so the data's index 0 is 1)
given adj : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(0..1)

$ c[i]: vertex i is in the clique (vertices indexed 1-based, so the output's first entry is vertex 1)
find c : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of bool

branching on [c]
$ the size of the clique, to be maximised
maximising (sum i : int(1..n) . toInt(c[i]))
such that
$ two distinct vertices both in the clique must be joined by an edge
forAll i : int(1..n) . forAll j : int(1..n) . ((i != j /\ c[i] /\ c[j]) -> adj[i,j] = 1)
